1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,384
Welcome to this series on
energy, fuels and calorimetry.

2
00:00:04,424 --> 00:00:10,072
Over these lessons we'll work out
where the energy in a fuel comes from,

3
00:00:10,112 --> 00:00:14,101
how we measure it, and how
to compare fuels fairly.

4
00:00:14,141 --> 00:00:19,078
Almost every calculation in the
topic starts in the same place:

5
00:00:19,118 --> 00:00:20,816
with amounts in moles.

6
00:00:20,856 --> 00:00:22,831
So that's where we begin.

7
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:26,284
Here's a quick check before we start.

8
00:00:26,324 --> 00:00:29,747
One flask holds one mole of hydrogen gas.

9
00:00:29,787 --> 00:00:32,449
Another holds one mole of water.

10
00:00:32,489 --> 00:00:36,249
Do they contain the same
amount of substance?

11
00:00:36,289 --> 00:00:38,529
Do they have the same mass?

12
00:00:38,569 --> 00:00:40,004
Pause and decide.

13
00:00:48,467 --> 00:00:54,700
They contain the same amount: one mole
each, so the same number of molecules.

14
00:00:54,740 --> 00:00:57,470
But the masses are very different.

15
00:00:57,510 --> 00:01:01,951
One mole of hydrogen gas has
a mass of about two grams,

16
00:01:01,991 --> 00:01:06,758
while one mole of water has a
mass of about eighteen grams.

17
00:01:06,798 --> 00:01:09,039
Same amount, different mass.

18
00:01:09,079 --> 00:01:14,374
Holding those two ideas apart is
the first skill in this episode.

19
00:01:14,733 --> 00:01:15,781
Here's the plan.

20
00:01:15,821 --> 00:01:19,452
We'll separate amount, mass,
volume and concentration.

21
00:01:19,492 --> 00:01:24,142
Then we'll use three relationships
that turn each of them into moles.

22
00:01:24,182 --> 00:01:28,900
We'll practise unit conversions, read
a balanced equation as a recipe,

23
00:01:28,940 --> 00:01:31,591
and finish with two practice questions.

24
00:01:32,533 --> 00:01:35,316
Picture a flask of liquid ethanol.

25
00:01:35,356 --> 00:01:39,302
We can describe the same
sample in several ways.

26
00:01:39,342 --> 00:01:42,955
Its mass, in grams, is
what a balance reads.

27
00:01:42,995 --> 00:01:48,601
Its volume, in millilitres or litres,
is how much space it takes up.

28
00:01:48,641 --> 00:01:54,536
And its amount, in moles, is a count
of how many particles it contains.

29
00:01:54,933 --> 00:01:58,914
A mole is a counting unit,
like a dozen, but enormous:

30
00:01:58,954 --> 00:02:04,424
about six point zero two times ten to
the power of twenty-three particles.

31
00:02:04,464 --> 00:02:09,636
Chemists count in moles because
reactions happen particle by particle,

32
00:02:09,676 --> 00:02:15,335
so the recipe of a reaction is written
in particles, and therefore in moles.

33
00:02:15,733 --> 00:02:18,348
Molar mass links mass to amount.

34
00:02:18,388 --> 00:02:23,410
It is the mass of one mole of
a substance, in grams per mole.

35
00:02:23,450 --> 00:02:27,808
For ethanol it's forty-six
point zero grams per mole,

36
00:02:27,848 --> 00:02:31,997
so forty-six grams of
ethanol is exactly one mole.

37
00:02:32,333 --> 00:02:38,052
Now let's swap the ethanol for the same
mass of water: forty-six grams again.

38
00:02:38,092 --> 00:02:40,146
The mass label is unchanged.

39
00:02:40,186 --> 00:02:44,634
But water molecules are lighter,
at eighteen grams per mole,

40
00:02:44,674 --> 00:02:49,720
so the same forty-six grams contains
about two point five six moles.

41
00:02:49,760 --> 00:02:51,854
Same mass, different amount.

42
00:02:52,200 --> 00:02:54,786
So these labels are not interchangeable.

43
00:02:54,826 --> 00:02:58,988
You cannot replace grams with
moles, or millilitres with litres,

44
00:02:59,028 --> 00:03:01,745
without a relationship that connects them.

45
00:03:01,785 --> 00:03:04,740
The next scene gives
you those relationships.

46
00:03:05,732 --> 00:03:07,278
First, from a mass.

47
00:03:07,318 --> 00:03:11,452
Amount in moles equals mass
divided by molar mass.

48
00:03:11,492 --> 00:03:12,787
Watch the units.

49
00:03:12,827 --> 00:03:14,206
Mass is in grams.

50
00:03:14,246 --> 00:03:16,877
Molar mass is in grams per mole.

51
00:03:16,917 --> 00:03:22,553
Dividing grams by grams per mole
cancels the grams and leaves moles.

52
00:03:22,593 --> 00:03:27,268
The units tell you the
relationship is set up correctly.

53
00:03:27,665 --> 00:03:29,367
Second, from a solution.

54
00:03:29,407 --> 00:03:32,342
Amount equals concentration times volume.

55
00:03:32,382 --> 00:03:37,349
Concentration is in moles per litre,
so the volume must be in litres.

56
00:03:37,389 --> 00:03:40,799
Then litres cancel, and
you're left with moles.

57
00:03:41,199 --> 00:03:42,416
Third, from a gas.

58
00:03:42,456 --> 00:03:44,790
At standard laboratory conditions,

59
00:03:44,830 --> 00:03:50,587
which in this course means twenty-five
degrees Celsius and one hundred kilopascals,

60
00:03:50,627 --> 00:03:55,196
one mole of any gas occupies about
twenty-four point eight litres.

61
00:03:55,236 --> 00:03:56,942
This is the molar volume.

62
00:03:56,982 --> 00:04:00,363
Amount equals gas volume
divided by molar volume:

63
00:04:00,403 --> 00:04:03,616
litres divided by litres
per mole gives moles.

64
00:04:03,999 --> 00:04:06,530
Two warnings about that last one.

65
00:04:06,570 --> 00:04:11,360
It applies only to gases, and
only at those stated conditions.

66
00:04:11,400 --> 00:04:17,126
A hot exhaust gas at six hundred degrees
takes up far more space per mole,

67
00:04:17,166 --> 00:04:21,918
so twenty-four point eight would
give the wrong answer there.

68
00:04:22,265 --> 00:04:23,614
Now a checkpoint.

69
00:04:23,654 --> 00:04:29,741
A solution has a concentration of zero
point one zero zero moles per litre,

70
00:04:29,781 --> 00:04:34,071
and you have two hundred
and fifty millilitres of it.

71
00:04:34,111 --> 00:04:40,035
Why can't you multiply zero point one
zero zero by two hundred and fifty?

72
00:04:40,075 --> 00:04:43,261
Pause and explain it in your own words.

73
00:04:51,665 --> 00:04:54,548
Because the concentration is per litre,

74
00:04:54,588 --> 00:04:58,521
the volume must be in litres
for the units to cancel.

75
00:04:58,561 --> 00:05:03,692
Two hundred and fifty millilitres
is zero point two five zero litres.

76
00:05:03,732 --> 00:05:07,740
Multiplying gives zero point
zero two five zero moles.

77
00:05:07,780 --> 00:05:12,986
If you multiply by two hundred and
fifty instead, you get twenty-five,

78
00:05:13,026 --> 00:05:15,534
which is a thousand times too big.

79
00:05:15,574 --> 00:05:21,530
The units left over, millilitres times
moles per litre, don't simplify to moles.

80
00:05:21,570 --> 00:05:24,194
That mismatch is your warning sign.

81
00:05:25,132 --> 00:05:29,707
Unit conversions cause more lost
marks than difficult chemistry,

82
00:05:29,747 --> 00:05:31,870
so let's make them mechanical.

83
00:05:31,910 --> 00:05:33,601
Here is a volume ladder.

84
00:05:33,641 --> 00:05:39,513
One cubic metre is one thousand litres,
and one litre is one thousand millilitres.

85
00:05:39,553 --> 00:05:43,231
Each step down the ladder
multiplies by a thousand.

86
00:05:43,598 --> 00:05:45,337
Here's a direction rule.

87
00:05:45,377 --> 00:05:51,416
When you move to a smaller unit, you need
more of them, so the number gets bigger.

88
00:05:51,456 --> 00:05:55,566
When you move to a larger
unit, the number gets smaller.

89
00:05:55,606 --> 00:06:01,719
For example, zero point zero eight five
zero cubic metres becomes eighty-five point

90
00:06:01,759 --> 00:06:05,836
zero litres, and then
eighty-five thousand millilitres.

91
00:06:06,198 --> 00:06:07,833
Mass works the same way.

92
00:06:07,873 --> 00:06:13,135
One kilogram is one thousand grams, and
one gram is one thousand milligrams.

93
00:06:13,175 --> 00:06:16,833
Energy too: one megajoule
is one thousand kilojoules,

94
00:06:16,873 --> 00:06:19,693
and one kilojoule is one thousand joules.

95
00:06:19,733 --> 00:06:24,478
So seventy-two thousand joules is
seventy-two point zero kilojoules.

96
00:06:24,865 --> 00:06:30,353
Time is the odd one out, because
its steps are sixty, not a thousand.

97
00:06:30,393 --> 00:06:35,079
One hour is sixty minutes, and
one minute is sixty seconds,

98
00:06:35,119 --> 00:06:39,085
so one hour is three thousand
six hundred seconds.

99
00:06:39,125 --> 00:06:42,850
Zero point one five zero
hours is nine minutes,

100
00:06:42,890 --> 00:06:46,095
which is five hundred and forty seconds.

101
00:06:46,465 --> 00:06:49,503
After every conversion, ask one question:

102
00:06:49,543 --> 00:06:52,882
should the number have
got bigger or smaller?

103
00:06:52,922 --> 00:06:58,177
If it went the wrong way, you multiplied
when you should have divided.

104
00:06:59,130 --> 00:07:01,440
A balanced equation is a recipe.

105
00:07:01,480 --> 00:07:07,386
Hydrogen burning in oxygen: two hydrogen
molecules react with one oxygen molecule

106
00:07:07,426 --> 00:07:09,441
to make two water molecules.

107
00:07:09,481 --> 00:07:11,570
Count the atoms on each side.

108
00:07:11,610 --> 00:07:16,562
Four hydrogen atoms and two oxygen
atoms before, and the same after.

109
00:07:16,602 --> 00:07:21,080
Nothing appears or disappears;
the atoms are just rearranged.

110
00:07:21,464 --> 00:07:23,502
Now scale the recipe up.

111
00:07:23,542 --> 00:07:28,005
Make it ten times bigger,
or a million times bigger,

112
00:07:28,045 --> 00:07:31,296
and the ratio stays two to one to two.

113
00:07:31,336 --> 00:07:36,406
Scale it up by a mole, and the
coefficients become amounts:

114
00:07:36,446 --> 00:07:43,287
two moles of hydrogen react with one mole
of oxygen to make two moles of water.

115
00:07:43,664 --> 00:07:46,522
So a coefficient gives a mole ratio.

116
00:07:46,562 --> 00:07:52,881
If zero point four zero moles of hydrogen
reacts, it needs half as much oxygen,

117
00:07:52,921 --> 00:07:58,999
zero point two zero moles, and it makes
zero point four zero moles of water.

118
00:07:59,039 --> 00:08:05,721
The ratio is valid because it comes from
counting particles, and moles are a count.

119
00:08:06,064 --> 00:08:09,635
But the coefficients are not a mass ratio.

120
00:08:09,675 --> 00:08:16,600
Zero point four zero moles of hydrogen
has a mass of only zero point eight grams,

121
00:08:16,640 --> 00:08:23,220
while zero point two zero moles of oxygen
has a mass of six point four grams.

122
00:08:23,260 --> 00:08:30,357
Mass is still conserved: zero point eight
plus six point four gives seven point two

123
00:08:30,397 --> 00:08:31,646
grams of water.

124
00:08:31,686 --> 00:08:36,118
But the masses are not in
a two to one to two ratio.

125
00:08:36,158 --> 00:08:41,489
Use the equation with moles, then
convert to grams at the end.

126
00:08:42,463 --> 00:08:45,798
One last tool before
the questions: rounding.

127
00:08:45,838 --> 00:08:51,498
Suppose an oxygen volume works out to
seventeen point seven six five litres,

128
00:08:51,538 --> 00:08:53,973
and you need the amount in moles.

129
00:08:54,013 --> 00:08:58,172
If you round that volume to
seventeen point eight first,

130
00:08:58,212 --> 00:09:04,287
then divide by twenty-four point eight,
you get zero point seven one eight moles.

131
00:09:04,663 --> 00:09:10,307
If you keep the full value instead,
you get zero point seven one six three,

132
00:09:10,347 --> 00:09:13,869
which rounds to zero
point seven one six moles.

133
00:09:13,909 --> 00:09:18,112
The early rounding changed
the third significant figure.

134
00:09:18,152 --> 00:09:22,169
In a longer calculation,
those small errors build up.

135
00:09:22,530 --> 00:09:25,767
So keep unrounded values
in your calculator,

136
00:09:25,807 --> 00:09:29,788
write the intermediate result
with a few extra digits,

137
00:09:29,828 --> 00:09:32,170
and round only the final answer.

138
00:09:32,210 --> 00:09:37,085
Base the number of significant
figures on the data you were given,

139
00:09:37,125 --> 00:09:38,614
which here is three.

140
00:09:39,597 --> 00:09:44,001
Here's practice question Q
zero one, worth four marks.

141
00:09:44,041 --> 00:09:49,474
Read it, then pause the video and
try it before I work through it.

142
00:10:01,930 --> 00:10:03,606
Let's read it together.

143
00:10:03,646 --> 00:10:07,261
We're given a mass of
ethanol and its molar mass,

144
00:10:07,301 --> 00:10:11,065
and a gas volume at standard
laboratory conditions.

145
00:10:11,105 --> 00:10:15,690
We're asked for two amounts in
moles, and then an explanation.

146
00:10:15,730 --> 00:10:18,788
Each part needs a different relationship.

147
00:10:19,130 --> 00:10:24,019
Before we calculate, what should
the ethanol answer look like?

148
00:10:24,059 --> 00:10:27,120
One mole of ethanol is forty-six grams,

149
00:10:27,160 --> 00:10:32,924
and we have only zero point three four
five grams, much less than a mole.

150
00:10:32,964 --> 00:10:38,291
So expect an answer far below one,
roughly one hundredth of a mole.

151
00:10:38,663 --> 00:10:41,679
Amount equals mass divided by molar mass:

152
00:10:41,719 --> 00:10:47,640
zero point three four five grams divided
by forty-six point zero grams per mole.

153
00:10:47,680 --> 00:10:52,781
The grams cancel, leaving zero point
zero zero seven five zero moles.

154
00:10:52,821 --> 00:10:57,590
That's under one hundredth of a
mole, which fits the prediction.

155
00:10:57,930 --> 00:11:01,071
For the gas, the volume is in millilitres,

156
00:11:01,111 --> 00:11:04,327
but the molar volume
is in litres per mole.

157
00:11:04,367 --> 00:11:05,615
So convert first.

158
00:11:05,655 --> 00:11:11,219
One hundred and eighty-six millilitres
is zero point one eight six litres.

159
00:11:11,259 --> 00:11:15,385
Then divide by twenty-four
point eight litres per mole.

160
00:11:15,425 --> 00:11:20,347
Litres cancel, giving zero point
zero zero seven five zero moles.

161
00:11:20,730 --> 00:11:23,978
So both samples contain the same amount.

162
00:11:24,018 --> 00:11:26,362
Does that mean the same mass?

163
00:11:26,402 --> 00:11:26,609
No.

164
00:11:26,649 --> 00:11:30,390
Equal amounts mean equal
numbers of particles.

165
00:11:30,430 --> 00:11:36,391
The mass also depends on molar mass,
and we don't know which gas this is,

166
00:11:36,431 --> 00:11:38,939
so we can't calculate its mass.

167
00:11:38,979 --> 00:11:44,569
Saying "we can't tell" here is a
correct chemical answer, not a gap.

168
00:11:44,930 --> 00:11:47,551
Compare that with a weaker answer: "No,

169
00:11:47,591 --> 00:11:51,713
because they are different
substances." It sounds reasonable,

170
00:11:51,753 --> 00:11:53,760
but it misses the causal link.

171
00:11:53,800 --> 00:11:58,605
Different substances matter here
because their molar masses can differ,

172
00:11:58,645 --> 00:12:01,374
and mass equals amount times molar mass.

173
00:12:01,730 --> 00:12:03,910
Here are the indicative marks.

174
00:12:03,950 --> 00:12:09,977
One mark for the ethanol amount, two for
converting the gas volume and dividing by

175
00:12:10,017 --> 00:12:16,159
the molar volume, and one for explaining
that equal moles don't imply equal masses.

176
00:12:16,530 --> 00:12:18,104
Now the tempting error.

177
00:12:18,144 --> 00:12:23,719
Dividing one hundred and eighty-six by
twenty-four point eight gives seven point

178
00:12:23,759 --> 00:12:24,842
five zero moles.

179
00:12:24,882 --> 00:12:29,965
The first wrong step is substituting
millilitres where litres are needed.

180
00:12:30,005 --> 00:12:35,369
A sanity check catches it: seven and a
half moles of gas would fill about one

181
00:12:35,409 --> 00:12:40,602
hundred and eighty-six litres, not one
hundred and eighty-six millilitres.

182
00:12:40,997 --> 00:12:42,193
Quick prediction.

183
00:12:42,233 --> 00:12:46,701
If the gas had been measured at
fifty degrees Celsius instead,

184
00:12:46,741 --> 00:12:50,337
could you still divide by
twenty-four point eight?

185
00:12:50,377 --> 00:12:51,468
Think about it.

186
00:12:57,930 --> 00:12:58,094
No.

187
00:12:58,134 --> 00:13:03,736
Twenty-four point eight litres per mole
applies only at twenty-five degrees and one

188
00:13:03,776 --> 00:13:05,096
hundred kilopascals.

189
00:13:05,136 --> 00:13:09,174
At a higher temperature, a mole
of gas occupies more volume,

190
00:13:09,214 --> 00:13:12,953
so you would need the
conditions-specific molar volume.

191
00:13:13,929 --> 00:13:17,644
Practice question Q zero
two is less guided.

192
00:13:17,684 --> 00:13:19,521
It's worth five marks.

193
00:13:19,561 --> 00:13:23,572
Pause the video now and
work through all of it.

194
00:13:38,995 --> 00:13:40,988
Part a is three conversions.

195
00:13:41,028 --> 00:13:44,255
Zero point zero eight
five zero cubic metres,

196
00:13:44,295 --> 00:13:49,772
times one thousand litres per cubic
metre, is eighty-five point zero litres.

197
00:13:49,812 --> 00:13:55,724
Zero point one five zero hours, times three
thousand six hundred seconds per hour,

198
00:13:55,764 --> 00:13:58,192
is five hundred and forty seconds.

199
00:13:58,232 --> 00:14:02,766
And seven point two zero times
ten to the power of four joules,

200
00:14:02,806 --> 00:14:07,266
divided by one thousand, is
seventy-two point zero kilojoules.

201
00:14:07,306 --> 00:14:11,331
Check the directions: smaller
units gave bigger numbers,

202
00:14:11,371 --> 00:14:14,420
and the larger unit gave a smaller number.

203
00:14:14,795 --> 00:14:16,748
For part b, first predict.

204
00:14:16,788 --> 00:14:22,956
Eighty-five litres of any gas at these
conditions is about eighty-five divided by

205
00:14:22,996 --> 00:14:27,248
twenty-five, so roughly three
and a half moles in total.

206
00:14:27,288 --> 00:14:30,007
Oxygen is only about a fifth of air,

207
00:14:30,047 --> 00:14:33,956
so expect roughly zero
point seven moles of oxygen.

208
00:14:34,329 --> 00:14:36,029
Only the oxygen counts.

209
00:14:36,069 --> 00:14:39,887
Its volume is twenty point
nine percent of the air:

210
00:14:39,927 --> 00:14:46,091
zero point two zero nine times eighty-five
point zero litres gives seventeen point

211
00:14:46,131 --> 00:14:48,511
seven six five litres of oxygen.

212
00:14:48,551 --> 00:14:52,143
Because all the gases
share the same conditions,

213
00:14:52,183 --> 00:14:55,738
a volume fraction is also
a mole fraction here.

214
00:14:56,129 --> 00:15:01,654
Then amount of oxygen equals seventeen
point seven six five litres divided by

215
00:15:01,694 --> 00:15:04,545
twenty-four point eight litres per mole,

216
00:15:04,585 --> 00:15:07,869
which is zero point seven
one six three moles,

217
00:15:07,909 --> 00:15:10,977
reported as zero point
seven one six moles.

218
00:15:11,017 --> 00:15:13,040
That matches the prediction.

219
00:15:13,395 --> 00:15:18,675
Marks: three for the conversions,
one for applying the oxygen fraction,

220
00:15:18,715 --> 00:15:21,148
and one for the amount of oxygen.

221
00:15:21,188 --> 00:15:26,692
The first trap is using the whole
eighty-five litres as if it were oxygen,

222
00:15:26,732 --> 00:15:29,764
which gives three point four three moles.

223
00:15:29,804 --> 00:15:33,585
The second is treating
cubic metres as millilitres,

224
00:15:33,625 --> 00:15:37,256
which makes the volume a
million times too small.

225
00:15:37,296 --> 00:15:41,192
In both cases, the prediction
would have warned you.

226
00:15:42,127 --> 00:15:43,832
Let's pull it together.

227
00:15:43,872 --> 00:15:47,701
If you have a mass, use
mass divided by molar mass,

228
00:15:47,741 --> 00:15:50,355
with grams matching grams per mole.

229
00:15:50,395 --> 00:15:56,575
If you have a solution, use concentration
times volume, with the volume in litres.

230
00:15:56,615 --> 00:16:00,520
If you have a gas at standard
laboratory conditions,

231
00:16:00,560 --> 00:16:04,692
divide its volume in litres
by twenty-four point eight.

232
00:16:04,732 --> 00:16:09,510
And a balanced equation gives
you mole ratios, not mass ratios.

233
00:16:09,861 --> 00:16:10,909
Closing recall.

234
00:16:10,949 --> 00:16:15,045
Without looking back: what
are the units of molar volume,

